{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The fix worked. Two weeks later, the same bug came back wearing a different hat</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A developer hits a <code>TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined</code>, pastes the full stack trace into an AI chat, and gets back a working diff in about ten seconds: add a fallback, ship it. It works. Two weeks later, a teammate hits what is structurally the same bug, data used before it's finished loading, in a different component, with a different error message. The first developer can't recognize it, because they never actually diagnosed the first one. They pasted a symptom and received a cure; the reasoning in between never happened where they could learn it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Instant-answer AI debugging optimizes the metric that's easiest to measure, time to green, at the direct expense of the one that's harder to measure and actually matters longer-term: whether the person doing the debugging built a reusable mental model of the failure. A diff that fixes the symptom without the person ever forming a hypothesis, testing it, and watching it confirmed is a fix with no attached learning event.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is the AI-assistant version of a well-known problem in worked examples research: handing a novice a fully-worked solution reduces the extraneous cognitive load of solving it themselves, which helps early on, but it also removes the retrieval practice and self-explanation that produce durable, transferable understanding. An AI debugging assistant that defaults to instant answers is, by design, always choosing the low-transfer path, because that's the path that resolves the current ticket fastest, not the one that improves the next ticket.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Ask before answering: guided questions instead of an instant diff</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A Socratic debug coach asks two to four guiding questions before it reveals anything: what did you expect to happen versus what actually happened, what's the last state you know was working, have you checked where the data actually comes from. Answering those questions forces the same diagnostic steps a senior engineer runs silently in their head, out loud, where the person doing it actually has to do the reasoning instead of skipping straight to the result.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The mechanism isn't "withhold the answer to be difficult," it's that each question targets a specific diagnostic move, expected-vs-actual narrows the search space, last-known-good isolates when the bug was introduced, "where does this value come from" traces data flow back to its source, that a fluent debugger already runs automatically. A user who answers these questions has, by construction, walked the same path a fluent debugger walks, whether or not the AI ever states the root cause explicitly.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The design has to include an escalation path, or it just becomes an obstacle: if two or three guided questions go nowhere (the user answers "I don't know" honestly, or the questions aren't converging), the coach should offer progressively more direct hints rather than stonewalling indefinitely. Pure withholding isn't pedagogically better than instant answers, it's just differently unhelpful; the actual goal is scaffolded struggle with a real floor under it, not struggle for its own sake.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The metric that went up while the skill went down</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team adopted an AI pair-programming assistant with instant-answer debugging as the default. In week one, "bugs resolved per week" went up sharply, every stack trace pasted in got a fix back in one turn. By week six, a senior engineer noticed something in code review: junior engineers submitting AI-suggested fixes couldn't explain why the fix worked when asked. The same categories of bug, kept recurring under slightly different symptoms, because nobody had actually learned to recognize the category, only to paste and apply.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team reconfigured the assistant so guided, question-first debugging was the default mode, and "just give me the fix" became an explicit opt-out a user had to ask for, rather than the default behavior. The immediate effect was that debugging sessions took longer per-incident, which the team expected and accepted. The effect they were betting on took three sprints to show up: junior engineers started catching and fixing the same bug category themselves, before ever opening the assistant, because the guided questions from prior sessions had actually built the recognition pattern.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      What made the reconfiguration defensible, and not just a vibes-based process change, was that they could point to a concrete mechanism: the guided-question sessions were functioning as spaced retrieval practice on the same handful of diagnostic moves, every session that made it to "where does this value come from" was rehearsing that specific move again, regardless of which bug prompted it. Instant-answer sessions had zero equivalent rehearsal built in; the fix was correct, but nothing about receiving it required exercising the skill the team actually needed more of.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

