{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Scaffolding, not stonewalling — and why this is a UX decision, not a prompt-tuning one</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Making a debug assistant "Socratic" isn't about writing a stricter system prompt that refuses to answer, it's a UX decision: what's the default mode, how many questions before a hint appears anyway, and is there always a visible "just show me" escape hatch so a genuinely stuck or time-pressured user isn't trapped. Get any of those wrong and the feature reads as the assistant being unhelpfully cagey instead of pedagogically useful.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The escalation ladder matters more than the first question does: question one, then a more specific question if that stalls, then a strong hint that names the area without naming the fix, then the full answer, is very different from three vague questions in a row followed by silence. Each rung should narrow the search space further than the last, or the user is just being asked the same question three different ways, which reads as friction, not guidance.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Current research on this exact interaction, LLM-driven Socratic debugging support for student programmers, is explicit that the hard part isn't generating a plausible-sounding question, it's generating one that's actually diagnostic for <em>this specific bug and this specific learner's stated understanding</em>, not a generic "have you checked your inputs?" that would apply to any bug. A production version of this feature needs the guiding questions grounded in the actual stack trace and the actual prior turns, the same discipline this project's own Casey hints are held to, or it degrades into the same recognizable boilerplate that defeats the point.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

