{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">Pasting a stack trace and getting a fix back in one turn feels efficient, but it skips the reasoning that would let you recognize the same bug next time. A coach that asks a few guiding questions first makes you do that reasoning yourself.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Default to guided questions (expected vs. actual, last known-good state, where the data comes from) before revealing a fix — make instant-answer an explicit opt-out, not the default, so the diagnostic steps actually get rehearsed.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Build a real escalation ladder — each unanswered question should offer a more specific hint than the last, with a visible "just show me" escape hatch — pure withholding is exactly as unhelpful as instant answers, just in the opposite direction.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
